    "q-163060": {
      "dom_id": "step-q163060",
      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "Consider the function $f(x,y)$ and the region $D$ given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=2x+3y,\\,D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 3y,\\,0\\le y\\le 2\\}.\n$$\n\nAccording to the mean value theorem for double integrals, there exists a point $(a,b)\\in D$ such that the volume of the solid enclosed between the surface $z=f(x,y)$ and the $xy$-plane over the region $D$ equals the volume of the right cylinder with base $D$ and height $f(a,b).$ Find $a$ given that $b=1.$",
      "worked_solution": "According to the mean value theorem for double integrals, there exists a point $(a,b)\\in D$ such that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A=f(a,b)\\cdot \\text{Area}(D).\n$$\n\nEvaluating the integral on the left-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{D}(2x+3y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{3y}(2x+3y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}[{x}^{2}+3xy]_{x=0}^{x=3y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}18{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =[6{y}^{3}]_{y=0}^{y=2} \\\\  & =48.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{3y}\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}[x]_{x=0}^{x=3y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}3y\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[\\frac{3{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{y=0}^{y=2} \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, we are told that $b=1.$ So, our initial equation simplifies to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}48 & =f(a,1)\\cdot 6 \\\\ 48 & =(2a+3\\cdot 1)\\cdot 6 \\\\ 8 & =2a+3 \\\\ 5 & =2a \\\\ a & =\\frac{5}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $(\\frac{5}{2},1)\\in D.$ Therefore, the answer is $a=\\frac{5}{2}.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
